.TH LONGWIRE-SWITCHBOARD 1 "November 2026" "longwire 0.5.55" "longwire manual"
.SH NAME
longwire-switchboard \- the longwire rendezvous server: gives nodes their numbers, introduces two nodes, then steps aside
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B longwire-switchboard
.BI \-\-registry " HOST:PORT/KEY" | none
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B longwire-switchboard token
.BR new " [" \fIN\fR "] | " list " | " remove " " \fITOKEN\fR
.RB [ \-\-tokens " " \fIFILE\fR "] [" \-\-state " " \fIDIR\fR ]
.br
.B longwire-switchboard bans
.BR list " | " clear
.RB [ \fIKEY\fR | all ]
.RB [ \-\-state " " \fIDIR\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The switchboard is the one public piece of a longwire deployment.
A node registers with it and is given a short number;
when one node dials another, the switchboard tells each the other's public key and addresses so that the two can punch through their NATs and talk directly.
After that the switchboard is not on the path: traffic never passes through it, and a call that is up survives the switchboard going down.
.PP
It sees that a node is online, its public address and port, who rings whom and when, and the names nodes chose to give it.
It never sees a frame, a seed or a private key.
.PP
The engine does the mechanism (sessions, presence, introductions); this script is the policy: who may register, and whose ring may go through.
.PP
.B \-\-registry
is required.
A private switchboard says
.B \-\-registry none
and keeps its derived code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " PORT|ADDR:PORT|[ADDR]:PORT"
Where to listen, over UDP (default 6741).
A bare port is IPv4 and IPv6 on any address;
.I ADDR:PORT
is IPv4 only;
.I [ADDR]:PORT
is IPv6 only; a comma separates several.
The switchboard also answers ECHO, over IPv4, on its echo port, the listen port + 2747 (9488 for 6741): the NAT-type check, and the probe of a node it is not answering.
Let both through a firewall.
The echo port answers each source address 8 times at once and then 2 a second, and everyone 1000 a second.
If that port is taken, it runs without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-tokens " FILE"
Registration needs a token: one per line,
.B TOKEN
or
.BR "TOKEN NUMBER" ,
where the second form reserves that number for that token.
A token is spent once the node it let in has its number: a late verdict or a failed registration leaves it good.
A token is visible characters with no spaces, quotes or backslashes, and does not begin with
.BR # .
Lines that are blank or begin with
.B #
are kept and are never tokens.
The file is rewritten whole beside itself and moved into place, so it must be in a directory the service can write.
Without this option, registration is open.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " DIR"
The state directory (default
.IR ~/.longwire/switchboard ):
the seed, the control socket,
.I numbers.log
and
.IR names.log .
.TP
.BI \-\-per\-ip\-hour " N"
Registrations per source per hour (default 20).
A source is an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64.
.TP
.BI \-\-rings\-per\-minute " N"
Rings per caller to one callee per minute (default 6); a callee takes five times that from everyone.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-nodes " N"
How many node sessions the worker holds at once (default 1024, at most 65536).
When they are all taken, a session that never registered gives way to a new one; a registered session never does.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-numbers " N"
The most numbers it will give out (default 100000).
Each is a bundle of about 1.3 KB in memory and a line in
.IR numbers.log .
.TP
.BI \-\-strikes " N"
Failures on an authenticated session, within
.BR \-\-strike\-window
seconds (default 600), that earn a ban (default 10; 0 turns bans off).
A refused registration counts two; a malformed registration, a dial faster than the limit and a dial of a number that does not exist one each; a control message over the session's budget a quarter.
A registration repeated within 5 seconds on one session is ignored, not counted, and a refusal because the policy script did not answer within 10 seconds is the switchboard's doing and is not counted either.
Nothing before the handshake ever counts: a spoofed address cannot get anyone banned.
.TP
.BI \-\-strike\-window " S"
Seconds over which strikes leak away (default 600).
.TP
.BI \-\-ban\-for " S"
The first ban of a key (default 900), doubling at each repeat.
.TP
.BI \-\-ban\-max " S"
The longest a ban gets (default 86400).
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-ban\-keys " N"
Different banned keys from one /24 (IPv6: /64) within the window that refuse
.I new
identities from that network (default 5); those already registered behind it are not affected.
.TP
.BI \-\-net\-ban\-for " S"
How long that network is refused (default 900).
.TP
.BI \-\-ban\-ceiling " N"
The most bans an hour (default 1000), so that a flood cannot make the switchboard refuse everybody.
.TP
.BI \-\-never\-ban " KEY|ADDR/PREFIX"
Never struck: a key id (64 hex digits) or an address with a prefix (192.0.2.0/24, 2001:db8::/32). Repeatable, at most 16. Nobody is exempt unless you say so.
.TP
.BI \-\-registry " HOST:PORT/KEY" "\fR | \fPnone"
The registry of switchboard codes, which gives this switchboard its short code.
.TP
.B \-\-issue\-codes
This switchboard is a registry: it gives codes to switchboards that register with it.
.TP
.BI \-\-public\-switchboard " KEY"
On a registry: the switchboard (an
.B lw1...
address or hex) that is given the code +1.
.TP
.BI \-\-codes\-per\-ip\-day " N"
On a registry: codes asked for per source per day (default 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-release\-after " TIME"
How long a number may go unheard before it is released, and held back as long again (default 365d; a suffix of s, m, h or d; a bare number is days).
Its owner coming back in that time gets the same number.
.TP
.BR \-\-caller\-id " " on | off
Carry the names callers announced (default on).
.TP
.BR \-\-directory " " on | off
Answer lookups for nodes that listed themselves (default on).
A node appears in either only if it asked to.
.TP
.BI \-\-group " NAME|GID"
Let that group use the control socket, mode 0660.
The service uses the group
.BR longwire .
Members of the group can read its status, page through its nodes, list its bans and watch the calls; they are not sent the events that carry a token, an address or a ban.
Only the user it runs as, or root, can stop it over the socket, decide its registrations or lift a ban.
Without this option only the user it runs as may use the socket; not even root may.
.SH TOKENS
.B token new
makes
.I N
registration tokens (one if omitted, at most 1000), appends them to the tokens file and prints them.
.B token list
prints the unspent ones, and
.B token remove
takes one out (a token with a reserved number goes by its first word).
They work on the tokens file only (mode 0600, rewritten whole beside itself), so no switchboard needs to run.
Without
.BR \-\-tokens ,
the file is
.IR SB_DIR/tokens .
For the service, name its file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
please longwire\-switchboard token new 3 \-\-tokens /var/lib/longwire\-switchboard/tokens
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run as root, it leaves the file owned by the owner of the folder it is in, so a service's file stays the service's.
The Switchboard window does the same from the desktop.
.SH BANS
.B bans list
prints, as JSON, the running switchboard's ban settings, its counters, each banned key with the seconds left and which ban in a row it is, and each banned network.
.B bans clear
.I KEY
lifts one key's ban (64 hex digits, or the first eight or more);
.B bans clear all
lifts every ban, the networks' too.
Bans are kept in memory: a restart forgets them.
.PP
Anyone who may use the control socket may list.
Only the user the switchboard runs as, or root, may lift.
The service runs as a transient user nobody logs in as, so lift its bans as root:
.PP
.nf
.RS
please longwire\-switchboard bans clear 0123456789abcdef
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B bans
finds the control socket in this order:
.BR $LW_SOCK ;
.I SB_DIR/ctl
(the switchboard run by hand as you;
.B \-\-state
moves it);
.I /run/longwire\-switchboard/ctl
(the service's).
A switchboard run by hand without
.B \-\-group
answers only the user it runs as; not even root may use it.
.PP
Each ban is one line of the switchboard's log, with the key's first 16 hex digits, how long, which ban in a row, why, and the command that lifts it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ban          key 0123456789abcdef... for 900 s (ban 1 in a row): registration refused   (longwire\-switchboard bans clear 0123456789abcdef lifts it)
.RE
.fi
.PP
A
.B net\-ban
line says that new keys from a network (a /24, or an IPv6 /64) are refused, and for how long; it names the network, never a node's address.
See
.IR /usr/share/doc/longwire/SWITCHBOARD.md .
.SH "RUNNING IT AS A SERVICE"
The package ships
.BR longwire-switchboard.service .
Put the arguments in
.I /etc/longwire/switchboard.conf
as one line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
ARGS="\-\-listen 6741 \-\-registry none \-\-tokens /var/lib/longwire\-switchboard/tokens"
.RE
.fi
.PP
and enable it with
.BR "systemctl enable \-\-now longwire\-switchboard" .
The service runs as a transient user with no capabilities, no home, a system-call filter, a memory limit and
.BR Restart=always .
Its state, including its identity, is in
.IR /var/lib/longwire\-switchboard .
Its control socket is
.IR /run/longwire\-switchboard/ctl .
Its log is the journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-u longwire\-switchboard" ):
the two lines it prints on start, and one line for each ban.
After the package is upgraded, restart the service: it keeps the engine it started with, its status does not say which, and the restart also forgets its bans.
Open UDP on the listen port and the one above it.
.PP
On start it prints its full address,
.BR lw1... ,
which is what nodes put after the slash in
.BR "\-\-switchboard HOST:PORT/KEY" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I SB_DIR/seed
The switchboard's identity.
Every node pins its address, so a lost seed means re-pointing every node.
Back it up and keep it private.
.TP
.I SB_DIR/numbers.log
.TQ
.I SB_DIR/names.log
.TQ
.I SB_DIR/codes.log
What the switchboard remembers: numbers, listed names, codes it has issued.
.TP
.I SB_DIR/ctl
The control socket.
.TP
.I SB_DIR/tokens
The tokens file of
.B token
when no
.B \-\-tokens
is given.
.TP
.I /etc/longwire/switchboard.conf
The service's arguments, one line
.BR ARGS=\(dq...\(dq .
.TP
.I /var/lib/longwire\-switchboard/
The service's
.IR SB_DIR ;
a link to
.IR /var/lib/private/longwire\-switchboard ,
which only root can enter.
.TP
.I /run/longwire\-switchboard/ctl
The service's control socket, mode 0660, group
.BR longwire .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SB_DIR
The state directory (the default of
.BR \-\-state ).
.TP
.B LONGWIRE_MOD
The module to load, when it is not on hibr's module path.
.TP
.B LW_SOCK
The control socket (default
.IR SB_DIR/ctl ).
.B bans
uses it when set, and otherwise looks for the service's socket too (see
.BR BANS ).
.SH SECURITY
A switchboard on the internet is open to anyone who can send a UDP packet.
It limits what a stranger can hold: sessions that never register give way, one registration is allowed every few seconds, dials are rationed, and the registry has an end.
Open registration can still be used up to
.B \-\-max\-numbers
by anyone with enough addresses; for a switchboard that matters, use
.BR \-\-tokens .
See
.BR longwire-security (7).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR longwire (1),
.BR longwire-profiles (5),
.BR longwire-security (7)
.PP
